{"version":19,"type":"full","exportedAt":1793145580604,"world":{"id":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"What the Dark Is Owed","description":"A gritty space opera in three eras. Lantern, a hollow-rock habitat beyond the Throat, keeps its air on credit: every breath is owed. Across seven centuries — the Closing, the Owing and the Healing — three sets of strangers fight the same debt.","coverImageId":null,"theme":"theme-scifi","continuityStaleThreshold":5,"calendar":{"week":{"days":["Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday","Saturday","Sunday"],"first":3},"leap":{"month":1,"days":1,"every":4,"skip":100,"keep":400},"startYear":2387,"yearSuffix":"CE","months":[{"name":"January","days":31},{"name":"February","days":28},{"name":"March","days":31},{"name":"April","days":30},{"name":"May","days":31},{"name":"June","days":30},{"name":"July","days":31},{"name":"August","days":31},{"name":"September","days":30},{"name":"October","days":31},{"name":"November","days":30},{"name":"December","days":31}],"eras":[{"label":"CE","counts":"up","first":1},{"label":"AC","start":2388,"counts":"up","first":1}]},"pinsSetClock":true,"createdAt":1793144674147,"updatedAt":1793144906847},"mapLayers":[],"locationMarkers":[{"id":"0nvafHfyRK1XBFvbSx62k","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Pumphouse","description":"Lantern's air plant and water stills, deep in the fore wall. Every scrubber and valve is keyed to the Tally.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"building","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145067327,"updatedAt":1793145067327,"version":1},{"id":"22kcZBjgWU3tyDAJK5eLn","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"Caul Deep","description":"The Company's deep ice mine on Caul. Shut down in the spring of 2387.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"dungeon","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145080142,"updatedAt":1793145080142,"version":1},{"id":"6WhnhSdLptwpOg_y6XG7V","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Covenant","description":"The Halden-Ash Company's last ship in the Sabra system, a long-haul carrier docked at Lantern's fore lock.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"building","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145086545,"updatedAt":1793145086545,"version":1},{"id":"AHMcYZIInNNvfUZfIUJri","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"Nhai","description":"The gas giant Lantern orbits. Banded ochre and bruise-purple, with a thin ring.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"region","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145084409,"updatedAt":1793145084409,"version":1},{"id":"GS98RIEmER9V5IIOiC590","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"Throat-Watch Station","description":"The Company's monitoring station near the Throat, which measures the wormhole's breathing.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"building","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145090824,"updatedAt":1793145090824,"version":1},{"id":"Ioi0lAWzNGfKuD6sO0y_s","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"Lantern","description":"A hollowed nickel-iron asteroid in orbit of the gas giant Nhai, spun for gravity, eleven kilometres long. Built by the Halden-Ash Company as the Sabra Concern's habitat. Lit from within by the Wick.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"city","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145056640,"updatedAt":1793145056640,"version":1},{"id":"LYsa4T85OiMWSOVa_8uQa","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Wick","description":"The fusion lamp that runs down Lantern's axis like a candle-wick: the habitat's sun. Tended from catwalks and crawls along the spine. Fed by fuel cassettes.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"landmark","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145058767,"updatedAt":1793145058767,"version":1},{"id":"Q5Iq2IntJfkK2QbC7YeH_","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Good Title","description":"A Throat-runner ship out of Port Ambry, mortgaged to Brightwater Recovery, captained by Mabry Ode.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"building","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145095120,"updatedAt":1793145095120,"version":1},{"id":"RgGiohTxgEkbv_a0TO0in","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Magpie","description":"Kas Ostrova's ice hauler: a battered tug with a tank-frame, on the Caul run.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"building","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145088692,"updatedAt":1793145088692,"version":1},{"id":"Ybosum8zedJ0zQI6eapQp","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"Port Ambry","description":"Concord frontier station on the far side of the Throat, built when the Throat was dead, waiting seven hundred years for it to heal.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"city","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145092993,"updatedAt":1793145092993,"version":1},{"id":"ZXX5Bvu5ikNR9QVw8Xe3x","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Upper Wards","description":"The best-lit terraces near Lantern's fore end, nearest the Counting House. Company staff housing in 2387; the counting families' quarter in the Owing.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"town","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145069455,"updatedAt":1793145069455,"version":1},{"id":"b2s6PBgzitLmRSeQ38clE","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Salt Docks","description":"The old ice docks at the aft end of the Dock Ring, half-abandoned, where the Salt land ice run off Caul outside the Tally.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"town","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145075859,"updatedAt":1793145075859,"version":1},{"id":"bpyo3ZRm53_YW7_EkVCbh","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Dock Ring","description":"Lantern's docking ring at the fore end of the rock, where the haulers come in from Caul. The Ardent Mercy's bell hangs at the inner lock, on the Bell Hook.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"building","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145063051,"updatedAt":1793145063051,"version":1},{"id":"fNC0HTz6yaBkVBuz8tMlw","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Throat","description":"The wormhole at the edge of the Sabra system, Lantern's only road to the Concord.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"landmark","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145082272,"updatedAt":1793145082272,"version":1},{"id":"fc68DOqhWtybQVCIoXxgn","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"Caul","description":"Nhai's ice moon. Lantern's water comes from it.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"region","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145078002,"updatedAt":1793145078002,"version":1},{"id":"jWi-Bx-VXJvuRZ3e3EWY5","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Counting House","description":"The vault at Lantern's core where the Tally engine runs, with the Company's offices above it. In the Owing it is the seat of the Count, built over the Hulk.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"building","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145065184,"updatedAt":1793145065184,"version":1},{"id":"nLnTyF9LPg8JbdZMm0i3Z","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Cold Wards","description":"Lantern's aft sections, abandoned, unheated and unlit: frozen streets, frost on the walls, and the dead of earlier centuries. Cutters strip them for metal.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"dungeon","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145071593,"updatedAt":1793145071593,"version":1},{"id":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Galleries","description":"The terraced housing rings carved into Lantern's inner wall, where the indentured live: bunk-rooms, chit-shops, pump galleries.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"town","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145060926,"updatedAt":1793145060926,"version":1},{"id":"pf2hnlYKmBENK4njkw23Q","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","mapLayerId":null,"linkedMapLayerId":null,"name":"The Hulk","description":"A ship-shaped mass embedded in the rock under the Counting House since the Closing. The Owing believe it is the Ardent Mercy, the ship that brought the first families.","x":0,"y":0,"imageId":null,"iconType":"building","tags":[],"factionId":null,"createdAt":1793145073723,"updatedAt":1793145073723,"version":1}],"characters":[{"id":"4DB-SYsFxGhwQg_T_P6Tr","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Wenna Blackwood","aliases":[],"description":"The Owing, 311 AC. Boss of the Salt, the smugglers who run ice off Caul outside the Tally. Sixty, one eye, a voice like a gravel chute.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144983312,"updatedAt":1793144983312},{"id":"4PJK2irFlLq0lPkroCgPG","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Teodor Basu","aliases":[],"description":"The Closing, 2387. Comptroller of the Halden-Ash Sabra Concern. Fifty-two, precise, soft-spoken, a stammer that comes out under pressure. He designed the Tally that keys Lantern's air to its debt, and he has done something worse.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144968427,"updatedAt":1793144968427},{"id":"8FkLsgdUH52stzy3qThJO","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Tamsin Hask","aliases":[],"description":"The Owing, 311 AC. A cutter: salvage diver in the frozen, unlit Cold Wards, stripping copper and steel from dead sections. Twenty-four, hard, funny when she forgets not to be. Born owing, like everybody.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144976526,"updatedAt":1793144976526},{"id":"B5N-ORRxzDAoRSjwt4RPw","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Ruth Achterberg","aliases":[],"description":"The Closing, 2387. Director of the Halden-Ash Company's Sabra Concern. Sixty. Not cruel for pleasure: she counts. Wears the Director's seal, a brass key-disk, on a chain at her throat.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144969746,"updatedAt":1793144969746},{"id":"CaEdi_nGwv_Yo9J6gmQoC","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Kas Ostrova","aliases":[],"description":"The Closing, 2387. Kasimir, Dagny's younger brother. Ice-hauler pilot on the Caul run, master of the battered hauler Magpie. Gambler, charmer, owes everybody. Loves his sister and nephew more than he can afford to.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144967080,"updatedAt":1793144967080},{"id":"CqIecEeYy-V9MMzz8K9l0","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Odile Marchbank","aliases":[],"description":"The Closing, 2387. Dockmaster of the Dock Ring and keeper of the Ardent Mercy's bell. Seventies, half-deaf, steady engraver's hands. Rings the bell for every death on Lantern and cuts the name into the bronze.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144972429,"updatedAt":1793144972429},{"id":"DdyKkgZFy6btLNqFcDspr","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Orrin Sallow","aliases":[],"description":"The Owing, 311 AC. A junior counter of the Counting House. Twenty-six, born in the Upper Wards to a minor counting family, believer in the Account's righteousness until he checks its arithmetic.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144980569,"updatedAt":1793144980569},{"id":"EEp34P_LIwiHnQUS72F_S","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Corvin Saal","aliases":[],"description":"The Healing, 3089 CE. Lien adjuster for Brightwater Recovery, bonded to the firm since he was nine for his father's water debt. Forty-four. Good at his job, which is making people pay.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144987343,"updatedAt":1793144987343},{"id":"K2sa5OCdLdT81u7a1yBt0","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Imre Vosk","aliases":[],"description":"The Owing, 311 AC. Comptroller-General of the Count, the Counting House's ruling body. Soft voice, perfect manners, holds the Count key and the lists.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144981954,"updatedAt":1793144981954},{"id":"L41M_slYybVBTVVnpviK7","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Fen Adaire","aliases":[],"description":"The Healing, 3089 CE. Provenance archivist; she reads old corporate records for a living. Twenty-nine. Owns, by family inheritance, a bronze ship's bell inscribed ARDENT MERCY, and wants to take it home to wherever home was.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144988698,"updatedAt":1793144988698},{"id":"LNs4_mqC3zge5HTKXOYFD","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Jory Hask","aliases":[],"description":"The Owing, 311 AC. Tamsin's brother, fifteen. A runner for anyone who pays. Light fingers.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144979214,"updatedAt":1793144979214},{"id":"MwmknvSbYe2Ci785A2xmS","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Lemuel Adeyemi","aliases":[],"description":"The Closing, 2387. A Gallery pump-fitter whose balance crosses the dimming line on the first page.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144975157,"updatedAt":1793144975157},{"id":"OQkh-QSkbPJM5F4BWfdhS","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Idris Kell","aliases":[],"description":"The Healing, 3089 CE. Sergeant of Brightwater's bailiffs aboard the Good Title. Professional, bored, dangerous.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144992785,"updatedAt":1793144992785},{"id":"QFvVlO7B_a1YmFMbnd84l","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Tol Brask","aliases":[],"description":"The Healing, 3089 CE. Engineer of the Good Title, Ode's man for twenty years. Talks to machines, not people.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144994122,"updatedAt":1793144994122},{"id":"TRqcAjx0xfYg8Pc46bEt2","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Quennell Haste","aliases":[],"description":"The Healing, 3089 CE. Junior partner of Brightwater Recovery, travelling with the claim. Courteous, tireless, legally exact.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144991462,"updatedAt":1793144991462},{"id":"U8Xy6zAdXRBSS6PGWXunZ","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Ninian","aliases":[],"description":"The Owing, 311 AC. The last master Wickwright, keeper of the dying lamp. Very old, nearly blind from the light he has spent his life tending.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144986013,"updatedAt":1793144986013},{"id":"WQ9NeOVAbNSpDXQKkGhBO","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Hale Varga","aliases":[],"description":"The Closing, 2387. Chief of Company Security on Lantern. Ex-Concord marine, broad, scarred, loyal to his men. His squad sealed something on Caul that he has not stopped thinking about.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144971118,"updatedAt":1793144971118},{"id":"WshcV6xq7C75QU8kONPBB","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Mabry Ode","aliases":[],"description":"The Healing, 3089 CE. Master of the Good Title. Sixty-three, gaunt, Throat-runner in her youth. Mortgaged her ship to Brightwater to make this run.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144990078,"updatedAt":1793144990078},{"id":"X1JRrGlyZdp8QBMZ6SYZN","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Brother Lusk","aliases":[],"description":"The Owing, 311 AC. The Bailiff of the Count: Vosk's enforcer, in grey, with a docket-baton.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144984690,"updatedAt":1793144984690},{"id":"g_pQemPTYKrSyuBXSXecB","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Joss Teague","aliases":[],"description":"The Closing, 2387. Wick crewman, Dagny's oldest friend on the spine. Big laugh, bad knees.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144973793,"updatedAt":1793144973793},{"id":"pDwz-N1w93a6lIKumMr7p","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Senna Hask","aliases":[],"description":"The Healing, 3089 CE. Reader of the Account on Lantern, who keeps the Tally in the Counting House.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144995526,"updatedAt":1793144995526},{"id":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Dagny Ostrova","aliases":[],"description":"The Closing, 2387. Wick-tender on Lantern's axial lamp and Speaker for the Wick crews. Forty-one, widowed, dry as rust, good with her hands and better with a crowd than she wants to be. Indentured since nineteen.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144964345,"updatedAt":1793144964345},{"id":"vOBEkx7GmTDi_KO4NiWCN","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Agathe Hask","aliases":[],"description":"The Owing, 311 AC. Tamsin's mother. Former pump-hand, now dying of lung-rot. Proud. Keeps the family's chits in a tin.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144977846,"updatedAt":1793144977846},{"id":"xBl28xa-sqvXJ5tgbrGuh","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Pim Ostrova","aliases":[],"description":"The Closing, 2387. Dagny's son, twelve. Quick, thin, collects bolts. Worships his uncle Kas.","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1793144965722,"updatedAt":1793144965722}],"items":[{"id":"6bdjOpq1CGsdTR3POKzFQ","imageId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"The Director's Seal","description":"A brass key-disk on a chain, the Director's half of the Tally's root keys. Engraved H-A.","iconType":"","tags":[],"version":1},{"id":"CR0TfKpzJ7APygVADXPUA","imageId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"The Count Key","description":"The key by which the Comptroller-General of the Count administers the Tally. Kept in the Hulk; never shown.","iconType":"","tags":[],"version":1},{"id":"J2rCe_zB9fHmaFmATwAUF","imageId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"The Tally","description":"The Halden-Ash ledger engine in the Counting House vault. Every lock, pump, scrubber and still on Lantern is keyed to it. It debits each indentured person for air, water and heat and credits their labour. A balance past the line is dimmed: doors close, rations stop.","iconType":"","tags":[],"version":1},{"id":"KjeFts84ONEtTsuUC_Ytl","imageId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"The Caul List","description":"Teodor's reconstruction, from payroll, of the names of the dead of Caul Deep. Paper.","iconType":"","tags":[],"version":1},{"id":"eDxCJE9t6GVv6E49wsVUt","imageId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"The Comptroller's Key","description":"Teodor Basu's half of the Tally's root keys: a slip of ceramic with a cipher burned into it, worn in his shoe.","iconType":"","tags":[],"version":1},{"id":"glZuTrPiRbVvp_16fPKY9","imageId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Fuel Cassettes","description":"Fusion fuel cassettes for the Wick, each the size of a coffin. Company property.","iconType":"","tags":[],"version":1},{"id":"lx0QoDRFeCbWybrVEqv8-","imageId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"Tally Chit","description":"A stamped metal token printed with a person's Tally number and balance, carried on a cord. Children get theirs at six.","iconType":"","tags":[],"version":1},{"id":"prExb-qfLBTTeQc6lJq0W","imageId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"The Mercy Bell","description":"A bronze ship's bell cast for the colony ship Ardent Mercy, hung at Lantern's inner dock lock on the Bell Hook. Rung once for every death on Lantern, and the dead's name cut into it: around the lip, then the waist, then wherever there is room.","iconType":"","tags":[],"version":1}],"characterSnapshots":[{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"CaEdi_nGwv_Yo9J6gmQoC","eventId":"A84nmiWcv6-wINQJ3D7BS","currentLocationMarkerId":"bpyo3ZRm53_YW7_EkVCbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"-UFXd6ArQYMDKJVxHV620","sortKey":1.0000000625,"createdAt":1793145335350,"updatedAt":1793145335350,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"g_pQemPTYKrSyuBXSXecB","eventId":"a8a4Mt67-JHW0f3gQMomY","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"7m9i-Hmp35a2FstoayCOf","sortKey":1.00000025,"createdAt":1793145335698,"updatedAt":1793145335698,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"g_pQemPTYKrSyuBXSXecB","eventId":"4FxCZy2Qs_KaAt_JBQN7T","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"G7AJYpF8NKIyIWc5i661I","sortKey":1.0000005,"createdAt":1793145335877,"updatedAt":1793145335877,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"JjF6UCjxigNDr2j96k1fL","sortKey":1,"createdAt":1793145334822,"updatedAt":1793145334822,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"CqIecEeYy-V9MMzz8K9l0","eventId":"A84nmiWcv6-wINQJ3D7BS","currentLocationMarkerId":"bpyo3ZRm53_YW7_EkVCbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"L9ZnZFl1SXxuHGEkfBxzf","sortKey":1.0000000625,"createdAt":1793145335283,"updatedAt":1793145335283,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"4PJK2irFlLq0lPkroCgPG","eventId":"a1u58gr6AFx6j7nnj_pXE","currentLocationMarkerId":"jWi-Bx-VXJvuRZ3e3EWY5","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"Lj_7PAZYxxiAe3fyAtvd3","sortKey":1.000001,"createdAt":1793145335994,"updatedAt":1793145335994,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"MwmknvSbYe2Ci785A2xmS","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"OUbw83NyyLMcN21u0ndA9","sortKey":1,"createdAt":1793145334885,"updatedAt":1793145334915,"version":2,"isAlive":false,"revived":false},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"xBl28xa-sqvXJ5tgbrGuh","eventId":"a8a4Mt67-JHW0f3gQMomY","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"OdjLl7_ZINxpTOm5fRpRh","sortKey":1.00000025,"createdAt":1793145335618,"updatedAt":1793145335618,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"xBl28xa-sqvXJ5tgbrGuh","eventId":"A84nmiWcv6-wINQJ3D7BS","currentLocationMarkerId":"bpyo3ZRm53_YW7_EkVCbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"U9WFfMyxep4aD-4ugmNuD","sortKey":1.0000000625,"createdAt":1793145335323,"updatedAt":1793145335323,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"CaEdi_nGwv_Yo9J6gmQoC","eventId":"1lGH_mKoG5xQJ7hU97kcj","currentLocationMarkerId":"bpyo3ZRm53_YW7_EkVCbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"W1HA3H178VUw0iS8FWuy8","sortKey":1.000000125,"createdAt":1793145335491,"updatedAt":1793145335491,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","eventId":"4FxCZy2Qs_KaAt_JBQN7T","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"WrIkKsvnv6SrGMEsXderP","sortKey":1.0000005,"createdAt":1793145335809,"updatedAt":1793145335809,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","eventId":"A84nmiWcv6-wINQJ3D7BS","currentLocationMarkerId":"bpyo3ZRm53_YW7_EkVCbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"a_VLvcgR1RSwdyqY9n8KW","sortKey":1.0000000625,"createdAt":1793145335273,"updatedAt":1793145335273,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"g_pQemPTYKrSyuBXSXecB","eventId":"l3mB0Z32LXliG1ks4H2N4","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"egDYt88iiIqub_vd-0BKD","sortKey":1.00000003125,"createdAt":1793145335152,"updatedAt":1793145335152,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"xBl28xa-sqvXJ5tgbrGuh","eventId":"1lGH_mKoG5xQJ7hU97kcj","currentLocationMarkerId":"bpyo3ZRm53_YW7_EkVCbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"koe_f7zXBqbLV2Ohd5lAc","sortKey":1.000000125,"createdAt":1793145335462,"updatedAt":1793145335462,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","eventId":"1lGH_mKoG5xQJ7hU97kcj","currentLocationMarkerId":"bpyo3ZRm53_YW7_EkVCbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"rEjbFoJY7WRogRODAGD0T","sortKey":1.000000125,"createdAt":1793145335451,"updatedAt":1793145335451,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"g_pQemPTYKrSyuBXSXecB","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"t-qipzwu7Fg7_Jnk3Vp-n","sortKey":1,"createdAt":1793145334834,"updatedAt":1793145334834,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","eventId":"a8a4Mt67-JHW0f3gQMomY","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"u0U-RCmF8jBb5_TGjvj8V","sortKey":1.00000025,"createdAt":1793145335601,"updatedAt":1793145335601,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"xBl28xa-sqvXJ5tgbrGuh","eventId":"4FxCZy2Qs_KaAt_JBQN7T","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"w3zGFBW5wJGvnwmKne34p","sortKey":1.0000005,"createdAt":1793145335842,"updatedAt":1793145335842,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","characterId":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","eventId":"l3mB0Z32LXliG1ks4H2N4","currentLocationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","currentMapLayerId":null,"id":"w843kNwjqq7HJqCG4UMJl","sortKey":1.00000003125,"createdAt":1793145335142,"updatedAt":1793145335142,"version":1}],"characterMovements":[],"itemPlacements":[],"locationSnapshots":[],"itemSnapshots":[],"itemCustody":[],"relationships":[],"relationshipSnapshots":[],"timelines":[{"id":"-ZiYa6rM8LYD4CUNIG4--","dayOffset":256522,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"The Healing","description":"","color":"#f87171","position":2,"createdAt":1793144830563,"version":5},{"id":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","dayOffset":67,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"The Closing","description":"","color":"#6366f1","position":0,"createdAt":1793144687136,"version":4},{"id":"bqr_Pql5DzQd25cWbMJih","dayOffset":113816,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","name":"The Owing","description":"","color":"#34d399","position":1,"createdAt":1793144795781,"version":5}],"chapters":[{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","number":19,"title":"Quit-Claim","synopsis":"At the mouth of the Throat, Dagny learns what Teodor's key really does, and chooses who Lantern will owe.","id":"1MW5Jge-H8rXVD55LzbJB","createdAt":1793144794665,"updatedAt":1793144794665,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","number":4,"title":"The Paired Keys","synopsis":"The Speakers put their demand to the Director and are refused. The comptroller who built the Tally tells Dagny there is a master key, and that it takes two.","id":"1mLWBC3drPbUeBuQy0FoX","createdAt":1793144788148,"updatedAt":1793144788148,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"-ZiYa6rM8LYD4CUNIG4--","number":6,"title":"The Throat","synopsis":"The race through the healing Throat. A rival ship dies in its lips. On the far side, the Sabra system is silent on every band.","id":"2Kh0EVXk-CjNvPK63bVhr","createdAt":1793144835013,"updatedAt":1793144835013,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"bqr_Pql5DzQd25cWbMJih","number":5,"title":"The Counter","synopsis":"A junior counter audits the Hask family for the Calling, finds the arithmetic wrong, and pulls the root of the Caul Account. Nobody holds it.","id":"3cwRgmDvjXK0JC6cuy4tY","createdAt":1793144823013,"updatedAt":1793144823013,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"-ZiYa6rM8LYD4CUNIG4--","number":21,"title":"The Names","synopsis":"Lantern comes out of eclipse. Nine hundred names, and a bell to ring for each.","id":"3gv96eDpK0doTU0ZB7JCo","createdAt":1793144841529,"updatedAt":1793144841529,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"bqr_Pql5DzQd25cWbMJih","number":11,"title":"Reserve C-V","synopsis":"Orrin decodes C-V. There is fuel, and the Count is keeping it. The first of the Called go into the cold.","id":"9aMzXwU26-kt3cR5nC-U4","createdAt":1793144825613,"updatedAt":1793144825613,"version":1},{"notes":"Kas says \"nine hundred on a shift\" and Teodor's file reads nine hundred: the only two hints at Caul before ch10. Keep them that quiet.","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","number":1,"title":"Air on Credit","synopsis":"A Wick-tender watches a man suffocate in a pressure lock because his balance ran out. The bell is rung. The Director announces the Throat will close in fifty-two days, and the indentured will stay.","id":"APRN5Ai8vKuZi7avjEG2x","createdAt":1793144687141,"updatedAt":1793145410788,"version":5},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"-ZiYa6rM8LYD4CUNIG4--","number":3,"title":"Provenance","synopsis":"At Port Ambry, where the Throat is healing, a bonded lien adjuster takes the job of collecting a seven-hundred-year-old debt. An archivist comes aboard with a bell covered in names.","id":"AqG5mFoTr2Pk939QIPRvQ","createdAt":1793144833696,"updatedAt":1793144833696,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"bqr_Pql5DzQd25cWbMJih","number":8,"title":"The Salt","synopsis":"Tamsin sells her hatch to the smugglers of the Salt and cuts it open. Inside: empty fuel racks, a manifest pointing to C-V, and names scratched in the dark.","id":"HDdL_ZxEKaitmru4YBzmU","createdAt":1793144824313,"updatedAt":1793144824313,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"-ZiYa6rM8LYD4CUNIG4--","number":12,"title":"The Hold","synopsis":"Fen breaks the throat-watch logs and finds nine hundred people who never arrived anywhere. Corvin opens the hold.","id":"K0CWXqGiSVJkflrWhfMhD","createdAt":1793144837613,"updatedAt":1793144837613,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","number":16,"title":"The Sale","synopsis":"The boarding of the Covenant is betrayed. Kas has sold the plan for two seats out.","id":"Qi1y2UB2l4ekV7FhtSWGo","createdAt":1793144793364,"updatedAt":1793144793364,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"-ZiYa6rM8LYD4CUNIG4--","number":18,"title":"Lien","synopsis":"Haste takes the ship. The captain dies. Corvin signs. On the bell's crown, words in another hand.","id":"QrN11_5DFiyFv_3faW44U","createdAt":1793144840213,"updatedAt":1793144840213,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","number":13,"title":"In Trust","synopsis":"Varga tells Dagny the Concord will not save them. Pim is dimmed. Odile engraves the dead of Caul. Dagny makes a new plan, and tells her brother.","id":"SF8tOubtPZvFrBVTjoVrR","createdAt":1793144792064,"updatedAt":1793144792064,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","number":10,"title":"The Shear","synopsis":"Teodor confesses Caul Deep. Kas flies Dagny to the sealed shaft. The Throat was never closing: the Company is closing it.","id":"Tcx46VR8E3-0Yl7l4nEsj","createdAt":1793144790765,"updatedAt":1793144790765,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"bqr_Pql5DzQd25cWbMJih","number":20,"title":"The Wick","synopsis":"The Called climb out of the cold and into the Hulk. Its name is still on its bridge.","id":"Vgnqq6wAe9CnM6uwe0cLv","createdAt":1793144829513,"updatedAt":1793144829513,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","number":7,"title":"Strike","synopsis":"The Wick crews dim the lamp. Security storms the spine and a man dies. Teodor brings Dagny the shape of the keys, and Odile asks for more bell.","id":"XQZwAfQA1gtIFkYZqNKyw","createdAt":1793144789448,"updatedAt":1793144789448,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"bqr_Pql5DzQd25cWbMJih","number":2,"title":"The Ghost Bell","synopsis":"Three centuries on, a cutter in the frozen Cold Wards finds a Company hatch. At home her mother coughs; the neighbours strike an empty bell hook for their dead. The Count proclaims the Calling.","id":"ddbkVvh0hPVMCNJMkEFYz","createdAt":1793144821697,"updatedAt":1793144821697,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"-ZiYa6rM8LYD4CUNIG4--","number":9,"title":"Dead Air","synopsis":"The Good Title finds the Company's dead throat-watch station. Haste explains what a lien attaches to. Something in the system is still counting.","id":"hXVlllW9-KUNOLinlW78k","createdAt":1793144836313,"updatedAt":1793144836313,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"-ZiYa6rM8LYD4CUNIG4--","number":15,"title":"The Far Side of Nhai","synopsis":"The bailiffs drill for consolidation. A sleeper dies in her pod. Haste seizes the bell as an asset of the Sabra Concern.","id":"lxDLW8gJeCpt1SBZcTHJv","createdAt":1793144838913,"updatedAt":1793144838913,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"bqr_Pql5DzQd25cWbMJih","number":14,"title":"The Wickwright","synopsis":"The last keeper of the lamp says it can be relit. Agathe's name goes on the list. The Salt names its price.","id":"rpwZrYK23uMW6acxfL-GH","createdAt":1793144826913,"updatedAt":1793144826913,"version":1},{"notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"bqr_Pql5DzQd25cWbMJih","number":17,"title":"The Calling","synopsis":"Orrin is struck from the books. Tamsin walks her mother into the Cold Wards.","id":"wm4uMrZt835CU2Jj7p-Z1","createdAt":1793144828213,"updatedAt":1793144828213,"version":1}],"events":[{"travelDays":0,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","chapterId":"APRN5Ai8vKuZi7avjEG2x","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","title":"The Magpie Comes In","description":"","locationMarkerId":"bpyo3ZRm53_YW7_EkVCbh","involvedCharacterIds":["qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","xBl28xa-sqvXJ5tgbrGuh","CaEdi_nGwv_Yo9J6gmQoC"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"id":"1lGH_mKoG5xQJ7hU97kcj","sortOrder":0.125,"createdAt":1793145334532,"updatedAt":1793145335402,"version":2,"remoteCharacterIds":[]},{"travelDays":0,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","chapterId":"APRN5Ai8vKuZi7avjEG2x","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","title":"Standing on a Table","description":"","locationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","involvedCharacterIds":["qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","xBl28xa-sqvXJ5tgbrGuh","g_pQemPTYKrSyuBXSXecB"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"id":"4FxCZy2Qs_KaAt_JBQN7T","sortOrder":0.5,"createdAt":1793145334271,"updatedAt":1793145335764,"version":2,"remoteCharacterIds":[]},{"travelDays":0,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","chapterId":"APRN5Ai8vKuZi7avjEG2x","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","title":"The Bell","description":"","locationMarkerId":"bpyo3ZRm53_YW7_EkVCbh","involvedCharacterIds":["qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","CqIecEeYy-V9MMzz8K9l0","xBl28xa-sqvXJ5tgbrGuh","CaEdi_nGwv_Yo9J6gmQoC"],"involvedItemIds":["prExb-qfLBTTeQc6lJq0W"],"tags":[],"id":"A84nmiWcv6-wINQJ3D7BS","sortOrder":0.0625,"createdAt":1793145334617,"updatedAt":1793145335210,"version":2,"remoteCharacterIds":[]},{"travelDays":0,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","chapterId":"APRN5Ai8vKuZi7avjEG2x","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","title":"The Clean Window","description":"","locationMarkerId":"jWi-Bx-VXJvuRZ3e3EWY5","involvedCharacterIds":["4PJK2irFlLq0lPkroCgPG"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"id":"a1u58gr6AFx6j7nnj_pXE","sortOrder":1,"createdAt":1793145333997,"updatedAt":1793145335932,"version":2,"remoteCharacterIds":[]},{"travelDays":1,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","chapterId":"APRN5Ai8vKuZi7avjEG2x","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","title":"Fifty-Two Days","description":"","locationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","involvedCharacterIds":["qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","xBl28xa-sqvXJ5tgbrGuh","g_pQemPTYKrSyuBXSXecB"],"involvedItemIds":["J2rCe_zB9fHmaFmATwAUF"],"tags":[],"id":"a8a4Mt67-JHW0f3gQMomY","sortOrder":0.25,"createdAt":1793145334394,"updatedAt":1793145335552,"version":2,"remoteCharacterIds":["B5N-ORRxzDAoRSjwt4RPw"]},{"travelDays":null,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","chapterId":"APRN5Ai8vKuZi7avjEG2x","title":"The Pressure Lock","description":"","locationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","involvedCharacterIds":["qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","g_pQemPTYKrSyuBXSXecB","MwmknvSbYe2Ci785A2xmS"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"sortOrder":0,"id":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","createdAt":1793144687168,"updatedAt":1793145334756,"version":8,"remoteCharacterIds":[]},{"travelDays":0,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","chapterId":"APRN5Ai8vKuZi7avjEG2x","timelineId":"TAAHx64wy6zcrgc0s_FVP","title":"Next of Kin","description":"","locationMarkerId":"obQHtZrWX1AjVLoialGbh","involvedCharacterIds":["qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","g_pQemPTYKrSyuBXSXecB"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"id":"l3mB0Z32LXliG1ks4H2N4","sortOrder":0.03125,"createdAt":1793145334695,"updatedAt":1793145334970,"version":2,"remoteCharacterIds":[]}],"travelModes":[],"timelineRelationships":[],"crossTimelineArtifacts":[],"mapRoutes":[],"mapRegions":[],"mapRegionSnapshots":[],"mapAnnotations":[],"loreCategories":[],"lorePages":[],"factions":[],"factionMemberships":[],"factionRelationships":[],"knowledgeFacts":[],"knowledgeReveals":[],"characterGoals":[],"sceneTexts":[{"id":"2-oFICcm3rVbfqBigPnaD","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"1lGH_mKoG5xQJ7hU97kcj","text":"The _Magpie_ came in an hour later with eighty tonnes of Caul ice in her tank-frame and a crack in her forward window that had been there so long it had a name. Her pilot came through the inner lock with his helmet under his arm and the whole of the Dock Ring's gossip already on his face.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Adeyemi. Gallery Ten.\"\n\n\"Lock?\"\n\n\"Lock.\"\n\nKasimir Ostrova closed his eyes for a moment. He was nine years younger than his sister and twice as handsome and he owed money, at a conservative estimate, to a third of the Dock Ring, none of which ever seemed to cost him a night's sleep. But he had grown up in Gallery Six the same as her, and he had seen the same locks.\n\n\"Uncle Kas,\" said Pim, and was swept up and hung upside down by the ankles, which at twelve he was too old for and permitted anyway.\n\n\"How's the run?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Cold. Long. The usual.\" Kas set Pim the right way up. \"Quiet out there, though. Quieter than it was.\"\n\n\"Quiet how?\"\n\n\"Caul Deep's still shut. You know they closed the Deep in the spring — the big shaft, the one that took nine hundred on a shift. Nobody's working it. Beacon's dark, gantries are up. Funny to fly past. Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city.\" He shrugged. \"They said the vein ran out. Sent all the Deep crews home through the Throat. Contracts bought out, every one. Lucky bastards.\"\n\n\"I never heard of the Company buying out a contract in my life,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Well. You never heard of a vein running out, neither. It's a season of firsts.\" Kas grinned at Pim. \"Come on. I'll buy you a noodle. I've been paid.\"\n\n\"You've been paid and you owe Marguerite at the noodle stall forty.\"\n\n\"Then I'll buy him a noodle and her a smile, and we'll all be square.\" He put an arm round his sister's shoulders and squeezed, briefly, hard. \"Sorry about Lemuel, Dag.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Me too.\"\n\nBehind them, Odile's engraver whined on in the bronze.","wordCount":361,"createdAt":1793145334541,"updatedAt":1793145334541},{"id":"4SKY7CAiMFblSt4xNwxw8","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"l3mB0Z32LXliG1ks4H2N4","text":"Joss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"","wordCount":252,"createdAt":1793145334702,"updatedAt":1793145334702},{"id":"60KHzXuQh2ewF3FhCDCJa","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"","wordCount":1168,"createdAt":1793145333989,"updatedAt":1793145334699},{"id":"7yD-i3iL0x6ny-qhuzU08","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"a8a4Mt67-JHW0f3gQMomY","text":"The announcement came at eleven the next morning, on every screen on Lantern at once.\n\nDagny was in the canteen on Gallery Six with Joss and two of the Wick crew, trying to work out how many hours the Speakers could find between them to stand the Adeyemi girl's debt, and getting a number that was not enough. The screen on the canteen wall was usually showing either the Company's safety notices or a football match from somewhere in the Concord that had been played eleven years before and brought through the Throat on a data-run. It went dark in the middle of a goal, and then it showed the Company's mark, the two letters H and A leaning on each other like tired men, and then it showed Ruth Achterberg.\n\nThe canteen went quiet in the way Lantern went quiet for the bell.\n\nThe Director was sitting at a desk with nothing on it. She was a lean, grey woman of sixty in a dark Company suit, and she wore at her throat, on a fine chain, the brass disk of the Director's seal, which every child on Lantern had been told was the key to everything. She did not look cruel. She looked like someone who had been awake all night doing arithmetic and had come out of it with an answer she disliked but would not apologise for.\n\n\"People of the Sabra Concern,\" she said. \"I am speaking to you today with news that affects every one of us.\n\n\"As you know, the Throat which connects this system to the Concord is not a fixed thing. It breathes. Its throat-watch station has measured that breathing for the whole of this colony's history. Three weeks ago the station recorded the beginning of what our physicists call a closing phase. Throats which enter a closing phase do not reopen on any timescale that is useful to us. The best estimate, from the Concord's own literature, is somewhere between six and eight hundred years.\n\n\"The Throat will remain passable for fifty-two days. After that, this system will be cut off.\"\n\nSomebody in the canteen laughed, the high bark of a person who has been hit. Nobody else made a sound.\n\n\"The Halden-Ash Company has a duty to its shareholders and to its staff,\" said the Director. \"It cannot maintain a concern which it cannot reach. It will therefore withdraw from the Sabra system before the Throat closes. The carrier _Covenant_, now at our fore dock, will carry essential staff and their families and such Company assets as can be moved. I have asked the department heads to prepare those lists.\n\n\"I want to speak directly to the indentured population of Lantern.\" She looked into the lens. Dagny would remember that afterwards: that she did not look away. \"Your contracts are held by the Company. The Company does not abandon what it holds. Your contracts will remain in force, in trust, for the full term of the closure, and will be honoured when the Throat reopens. Lantern's systems — its air, its water, its heat, its light — will continue to be administered by the Tally, which has kept you safe for ninety years and which will keep you safe after we are gone. The Tally does not need us. It needs only that you go on working, as you always have, and it will go on keeping you alive.\n\n\"I know that this is hard news. I wish that I had better. Thank you.\"\n\nThe screen went back to the Company's mark. Then, after a moment, to the football match, which had been paused at the instant before the goal and now went on as if nothing had happened, the ball crossing the line, the crowd eleven years ago and four hundred light-years away going wild.","wordCount":636,"createdAt":1793145334407,"updatedAt":1793145334407},{"id":"OeBzEZ_3vXNpt_vM9hAbb","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"A84nmiWcv6-wINQJ3D7BS","text":"The bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"","wordCount":571,"createdAt":1793145334628,"updatedAt":1793145334628},{"id":"gATg08Lbd7eU3yzW7E0lZ","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"4FxCZy2Qs_KaAt_JBQN7T","text":"What happened in the canteen in the next ten minutes Dagny remembered afterwards only in pieces. A man on Gallery Six put his chair through the screen and the screen went on showing football in a web of cracks. Two women began to fight over nothing, over a bench, and had to be pulled apart. Old Mister Okonkwo the schoolteacher sat with his hands flat on the table, saying, \"Six hundred years, six hundred years,\" as if it were a sum that would come out differently if he said it often enough. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the fuel. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the water. Somebody shouted that they should go up to the Upper Wards now, all of them, and see how many lists the department heads had made when they had their own windows broken.\n\nAnd Pim was beside her, she didn't know when he had come in, saying, \"Mum. Mum. In trust. What's in trust mean?\"\n\nShe understood it, she realised, before she understood anything else. It was Lantern; you learned the Company's language before you learned your prayers. _Held in trust_ meant that the Company was leaving, and the debt was staying. It meant that every chit on every wrist on Lantern would go on counting down, hour by hour, against the air, for six hundred years, and that if the Throat ever opened again somebody in a Company suit would come through it with the books in their hand to see how much was owed. It meant that the Adeyemi girl's twelve hundred would be eighteen hundred by the time she was grown and nine thousand by the time she died, and would go to whoever came after her.\n\nIt meant that the lock on Gallery Nine would go on doing what it had done to Lemuel, with nobody even in the Counting House to blame for it.\n\nShe found she was standing on a table. She did not remember getting up there. The canteen was looking at her, the way the Wick crews looked at her when the lamp went wrong and someone had to say a number out loud.\n\n\"All right,\" Dagny said. Her voice came out level, which surprised her. \"All right. Shut up and listen. I'm a Speaker for the Wick crews, and from tonight the Wick crews are going to want to talk to the other crews. Pumps, farms, docks, stills. Everyone who wears a chit. Fifty-two days is a long time. It's long enough for the Company to pack. It's long enough for us to make it listen first.\"\n\n\"Listen to what?\" somebody shouted.\n\n\"To the one thing it hasn't said.\" She held up her wrist, with the chit on it, so that the canteen could see the blue numbers. \"She said the Tally keeps us alive. It doesn't. We keep it alive. We fed it ninety years. If the Company's going, the Company can take its ledger with it, or it can switch the bloody thing off before it goes. Either way, nobody on this rock is going to owe air to an empty office for six hundred years.\"\n\nThere was a silence. Then the man who had broken the screen began to clap, slowly, and others took it up; and Joss, below her, looked up with his wet red face and said, very quietly, so that only she could hear: \"Dag. You know what you just did.\"\n\nShe did. She had just volunteered.","wordCount":581,"createdAt":1793145334287,"updatedAt":1793145334287},{"id":"xrySoxJIxKbbPxC0x8-P2","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"a1u58gr6AFx6j7nnj_pXE","text":"On the top floor of the Counting House, behind the only clean window on Lantern, Teodor Basu, Comptroller of the Sabra Concern, watched the feed from the Gallery Six canteen camera with the sound off. He watched the woman on the table. He watched her hold up her wrist.\n\nHe ran her number. Ostrova, Dagny. Wick crews. Indentured nineteen years. Balance minus six hundred and eighty-one, including a tampering fee of two hundred posted last night at Lock Nine-Forty.\n\nHis hand, on the desk, was shaking. He put the other one over it until it stopped.\n\nThen he opened a file that was not on any list the Director had asked for, and looked for a long time at a number that was not a balance at all, and was nine hundred, and closed it again.","wordCount":136,"createdAt":1793145334007,"updatedAt":1793145334007}],"plotThreads":[],"motifs":[],"continuitySuppressions":[],"writingLogs":[{"id":"wg0crDNUkJBKFqJx83ah3","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","date":"2026-10-27","words":3803,"createdAt":1793145333989,"updatedAt":1793145334688}],"sceneRevisions":[{"id":"5NuGPt1mApNNHutJ4ZF_M","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n## Next of Kin\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"\n\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Odile Marchbank @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nItems: ^The Mercy Bell\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"","wordCount":2030,"createdAt":1793145334622},{"id":"93IylJn-MVYi5wYRqfsA7","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n## Next of Kin\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"\n\n## The Bell\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Odile Marchbank @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nItems: ^The Mercy Bell\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"\n\n## The Magpie Comes In\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe _Magpie_ came in an hour later with eighty tonnes of Caul ice in her tank-frame and a crack in her forward window that had been there so long it had a name. Her pilot came through the inner lock with his helmet under his arm and the whole of the Dock Ring's gossip already on his face.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Adeyemi. Gallery Ten.\"\n\n\"Lock?\"\n\n\"Lock.\"\n\nKasimir Ostrova closed his eyes for a moment. He was nine years younger than his sister and twice as handsome and he owed money, at a conservative estimate, to a third of the Dock Ring, none of which ever seemed to cost him a night's sleep. But he had grown up in Gallery Six the same as her, and he had seen the same locks.\n\n\"Uncle Kas,\" said Pim, and was swept up and hung upside down by the ankles, which at twelve he was too old for and permitted anyway.\n\n\"How's the run?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Cold. Long. The usual.\" Kas set Pim the right way up. \"Quiet out there, though. Quieter than it was.\"\n\n\"Quiet how?\"\n\n\"Caul Deep's still shut. You know they closed the Deep in the spring — the big shaft, the one that took nine hundred on a shift. Nobody's working it. Beacon's dark, gantries are up. Funny to fly past. Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city.\" He shrugged. \"They said the vein ran out. Sent all the Deep crews home through the Throat. Contracts bought out, every one. Lucky bastards.\"\n\n\"I never heard of the Company buying out a contract in my life,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Well. You never heard of a vein running out, neither. It's a season of firsts.\" Kas grinned at Pim. \"Come on. I'll buy you a noodle. I've been paid.\"\n\n\"You've been paid and you owe Marguerite at the noodle stall forty.\"\n\n\"Then I'll buy him a noodle and her a smile, and we'll all be square.\" He put an arm round his sister's shoulders and squeezed, briefly, hard. \"Sorry about Lemuel, Dag.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Me too.\"\n\nBehind them, Odile's engraver whined on in the bronze.\n\n## Fifty-Two Days\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Joss Teague @@Ruth Achterberg:phone\nItems: ^The Tally\nWhen: +1 day\n]\n\nThe announcement came at eleven the next morning, on every screen on Lantern at once.\n\nDagny was in the canteen on Gallery Six with Joss and two of the Wick crew, trying to work out how many hours the Speakers could find between them to stand the Adeyemi girl's debt, and getting a number that was not enough. The screen on the canteen wall was usually showing either the Company's safety notices or a football match from somewhere in the Concord that had been played eleven years before and brought through the Throat on a data-run. It went dark in the middle of a goal, and then it showed the Company's mark, the two letters H and A leaning on each other like tired men, and then it showed Ruth Achterberg.\n\nThe canteen went quiet in the way Lantern went quiet for the bell.\n\nThe Director was sitting at a desk with nothing on it. She was a lean, grey woman of sixty in a dark Company suit, and she wore at her throat, on a fine chain, the brass disk of the Director's seal, which every child on Lantern had been told was the key to everything. She did not look cruel. She looked like someone who had been awake all night doing arithmetic and had come out of it with an answer she disliked but would not apologise for.\n\n\"People of the Sabra Concern,\" she said. \"I am speaking to you today with news that affects every one of us.\n\n\"As you know, the Throat which connects this system to the Concord is not a fixed thing. It breathes. Its throat-watch station has measured that breathing for the whole of this colony's history. Three weeks ago the station recorded the beginning of what our physicists call a closing phase. Throats which enter a closing phase do not reopen on any timescale that is useful to us. The best estimate, from the Concord's own literature, is somewhere between six and eight hundred years.\n\n\"The Throat will remain passable for fifty-two days. After that, this system will be cut off.\"\n\nSomebody in the canteen laughed, the high bark of a person who has been hit. Nobody else made a sound.\n\n\"The Halden-Ash Company has a duty to its shareholders and to its staff,\" said the Director. \"It cannot maintain a concern which it cannot reach. It will therefore withdraw from the Sabra system before the Throat closes. The carrier _Covenant_, now at our fore dock, will carry essential staff and their families and such Company assets as can be moved. I have asked the department heads to prepare those lists.\n\n\"I want to speak directly to the indentured population of Lantern.\" She looked into the lens. Dagny would remember that afterwards: that she did not look away. \"Your contracts are held by the Company. The Company does not abandon what it holds. Your contracts will remain in force, in trust, for the full term of the closure, and will be honoured when the Throat reopens. Lantern's systems — its air, its water, its heat, its light — will continue to be administered by the Tally, which has kept you safe for ninety years and which will keep you safe after we are gone. The Tally does not need us. It needs only that you go on working, as you always have, and it will go on keeping you alive.\n\n\"I know that this is hard news. I wish that I had better. Thank you.\"\n\nThe screen went back to the Company's mark. Then, after a moment, to the football match, which had been paused at the instant before the goal and now went on as if nothing had happened, the ball crossing the line, the crowd eleven years ago and four hundred light-years away going wild.\n\n## Standing on a Table\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nWhat happened in the canteen in the next ten minutes Dagny remembered afterwards only in pieces. A man on Gallery Six put his chair through the screen and the screen went on showing football in a web of cracks. Two women began to fight over nothing, over a bench, and had to be pulled apart. Old Mister Okonkwo the schoolteacher sat with his hands flat on the table, saying, \"Six hundred years, six hundred years,\" as if it were a sum that would come out differently if he said it often enough. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the fuel. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the water. Somebody shouted that they should go up to the Upper Wards now, all of them, and see how many lists the department heads had made when they had their own windows broken.\n\nAnd Pim was beside her, she didn't know when he had come in, saying, \"Mum. Mum. In trust. What's in trust mean?\"\n\nShe understood it, she realised, before she understood anything else. It was Lantern; you learned the Company's language before you learned your prayers. _Held in trust_ meant that the Company was leaving, and the debt was staying. It meant that every chit on every wrist on Lantern would go on counting down, hour by hour, against the air, for six hundred years, and that if the Throat ever opened again somebody in a Company suit would come through it with the books in their hand to see how much was owed. It meant that the Adeyemi girl's twelve hundred would be eighteen hundred by the time she was grown and nine thousand by the time she died, and would go to whoever came after her.\n\nIt meant that the lock on Gallery Nine would go on doing what it had done to Lemuel, with nobody even in the Counting House to blame for it.\n\nShe found she was standing on a table. She did not remember getting up there. The canteen was looking at her, the way the Wick crews looked at her when the lamp went wrong and someone had to say a number out loud.\n\n\"All right,\" Dagny said. Her voice came out level, which surprised her. \"All right. Shut up and listen. I'm a Speaker for the Wick crews, and from tonight the Wick crews are going to want to talk to the other crews. Pumps, farms, docks, stills. Everyone who wears a chit. Fifty-two days is a long time. It's long enough for the Company to pack. It's long enough for us to make it listen first.\"\n\n\"Listen to what?\" somebody shouted.\n\n\"To the one thing it hasn't said.\" She held up her wrist, with the chit on it, so that the canteen could see the blue numbers. \"She said the Tally keeps us alive. It doesn't. We keep it alive. We fed it ninety years. If the Company's going, the Company can take its ledger with it, or it can switch the bloody thing off before it goes. Either way, nobody on this rock is going to owe air to an empty office for six hundred years.\"\n\nThere was a silence. Then the man who had broken the screen began to clap, slowly, and others took it up; and Joss, below her, looked up with his wet red face and said, very quietly, so that only she could hear: \"Dag. You know what you just did.\"\n\nShe did. She had just volunteered.\n\n[\nPlace: #The Counting House\nCharacters: @@Teodor Basu\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nOn the top floor of the Counting House, behind the only clean window on Lantern, Teodor Basu, Comptroller of the Sabra Concern, watched the feed from the Gallery Six canteen camera with the sound off. He watched the woman on the table. He watched her hold up her wrist.\n\nHe ran her number. Ostrova, Dagny. Wick crews. Indentured nineteen years. Balance minus six hundred and eighty-one, including a tampering fee of two hundred posted last night at Lock Nine-Forty.\n\nHis hand, on the desk, was shaking. He put the other one over it until it stopped.\n\nThen he opened a file that was not on any list the Director had asked for, and looked for a long time at a number that was not a balance at all, and was nine hundred, and closed it again.","wordCount":3823,"createdAt":1793145334003},{"id":"MaMC8fahlujHTcQXtDprm","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n## Next of Kin\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"\n\n## The Bell\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Odile Marchbank @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nItems: ^The Mercy Bell\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"\n\n## The Magpie Comes In\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe _Magpie_ came in an hour later with eighty tonnes of Caul ice in her tank-frame and a crack in her forward window that had been there so long it had a name. Her pilot came through the inner lock with his helmet under his arm and the whole of the Dock Ring's gossip already on his face.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Adeyemi. Gallery Ten.\"\n\n\"Lock?\"\n\n\"Lock.\"\n\nKasimir Ostrova closed his eyes for a moment. He was nine years younger than his sister and twice as handsome and he owed money, at a conservative estimate, to a third of the Dock Ring, none of which ever seemed to cost him a night's sleep. But he had grown up in Gallery Six the same as her, and he had seen the same locks.\n\n\"Uncle Kas,\" said Pim, and was swept up and hung upside down by the ankles, which at twelve he was too old for and permitted anyway.\n\n\"How's the run?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Cold. Long. The usual.\" Kas set Pim the right way up. \"Quiet out there, though. Quieter than it was.\"\n\n\"Quiet how?\"\n\n\"Caul Deep's still shut. You know they closed the Deep in the spring — the big shaft, the one that took nine hundred on a shift. Nobody's working it. Beacon's dark, gantries are up. Funny to fly past. Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city.\" He shrugged. \"They said the vein ran out. Sent all the Deep crews home through the Throat. Contracts bought out, every one. Lucky bastards.\"\n\n\"I never heard of the Company buying out a contract in my life,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Well. You never heard of a vein running out, neither. It's a season of firsts.\" Kas grinned at Pim. \"Come on. I'll buy you a noodle. I've been paid.\"\n\n\"You've been paid and you owe Marguerite at the noodle stall forty.\"\n\n\"Then I'll buy him a noodle and her a smile, and we'll all be square.\" He put an arm round his sister's shoulders and squeezed, briefly, hard. \"Sorry about Lemuel, Dag.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Me too.\"\n\nBehind them, Odile's engraver whined on in the bronze.\n\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Joss Teague @@Ruth Achterberg:phone\nItems: ^The Tally\nWhen: +1 day\n]\n\nThe announcement came at eleven the next morning, on every screen on Lantern at once.\n\nDagny was in the canteen on Gallery Six with Joss and two of the Wick crew, trying to work out how many hours the Speakers could find between them to stand the Adeyemi girl's debt, and getting a number that was not enough. The screen on the canteen wall was usually showing either the Company's safety notices or a football match from somewhere in the Concord that had been played eleven years before and brought through the Throat on a data-run. It went dark in the middle of a goal, and then it showed the Company's mark, the two letters H and A leaning on each other like tired men, and then it showed Ruth Achterberg.\n\nThe canteen went quiet in the way Lantern went quiet for the bell.\n\nThe Director was sitting at a desk with nothing on it. She was a lean, grey woman of sixty in a dark Company suit, and she wore at her throat, on a fine chain, the brass disk of the Director's seal, which every child on Lantern had been told was the key to everything. She did not look cruel. She looked like someone who had been awake all night doing arithmetic and had come out of it with an answer she disliked but would not apologise for.\n\n\"People of the Sabra Concern,\" she said. \"I am speaking to you today with news that affects every one of us.\n\n\"As you know, the Throat which connects this system to the Concord is not a fixed thing. It breathes. Its throat-watch station has measured that breathing for the whole of this colony's history. Three weeks ago the station recorded the beginning of what our physicists call a closing phase. Throats which enter a closing phase do not reopen on any timescale that is useful to us. The best estimate, from the Concord's own literature, is somewhere between six and eight hundred years.\n\n\"The Throat will remain passable for fifty-two days. After that, this system will be cut off.\"\n\nSomebody in the canteen laughed, the high bark of a person who has been hit. Nobody else made a sound.\n\n\"The Halden-Ash Company has a duty to its shareholders and to its staff,\" said the Director. \"It cannot maintain a concern which it cannot reach. It will therefore withdraw from the Sabra system before the Throat closes. The carrier _Covenant_, now at our fore dock, will carry essential staff and their families and such Company assets as can be moved. I have asked the department heads to prepare those lists.\n\n\"I want to speak directly to the indentured population of Lantern.\" She looked into the lens. Dagny would remember that afterwards: that she did not look away. \"Your contracts are held by the Company. The Company does not abandon what it holds. Your contracts will remain in force, in trust, for the full term of the closure, and will be honoured when the Throat reopens. Lantern's systems — its air, its water, its heat, its light — will continue to be administered by the Tally, which has kept you safe for ninety years and which will keep you safe after we are gone. The Tally does not need us. It needs only that you go on working, as you always have, and it will go on keeping you alive.\n\n\"I know that this is hard news. I wish that I had better. Thank you.\"\n\nThe screen went back to the Company's mark. Then, after a moment, to the football match, which had been paused at the instant before the goal and now went on as if nothing had happened, the ball crossing the line, the crowd eleven years ago and four hundred light-years away going wild.","wordCount":3071,"createdAt":1793145334401},{"id":"RGiIevFhwkzS3Q5gZj6md","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n## Next of Kin\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"\n\n## The Bell\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Odile Marchbank @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nItems: ^The Mercy Bell\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"\n\n## The Magpie Comes In\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe _Magpie_ came in an hour later with eighty tonnes of Caul ice in her tank-frame and a crack in her forward window that had been there so long it had a name. Her pilot came through the inner lock with his helmet under his arm and the whole of the Dock Ring's gossip already on his face.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Adeyemi. Gallery Ten.\"\n\n\"Lock?\"\n\n\"Lock.\"\n\nKasimir Ostrova closed his eyes for a moment. He was nine years younger than his sister and twice as handsome and he owed money, at a conservative estimate, to a third of the Dock Ring, none of which ever seemed to cost him a night's sleep. But he had grown up in Gallery Six the same as her, and he had seen the same locks.\n\n\"Uncle Kas,\" said Pim, and was swept up and hung upside down by the ankles, which at twelve he was too old for and permitted anyway.\n\n\"How's the run?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Cold. Long. The usual.\" Kas set Pim the right way up. \"Quiet out there, though. Quieter than it was.\"\n\n\"Quiet how?\"\n\n\"Caul Deep's still shut. You know they closed the Deep in the spring — the big shaft, the one that took nine hundred on a shift. Nobody's working it. Beacon's dark, gantries are up. Funny to fly past. Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city.\" He shrugged. \"They said the vein ran out. Sent all the Deep crews home through the Throat. Contracts bought out, every one. Lucky bastards.\"\n\n\"I never heard of the Company buying out a contract in my life,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Well. You never heard of a vein running out, neither. It's a season of firsts.\" Kas grinned at Pim. \"Come on. I'll buy you a noodle. I've been paid.\"\n\n\"You've been paid and you owe Marguerite at the noodle stall forty.\"\n\n\"Then I'll buy him a noodle and her a smile, and we'll all be square.\" He put an arm round his sister's shoulders and squeezed, briefly, hard. \"Sorry about Lemuel, Dag.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Me too.\"\n\nBehind them, Odile's engraver whined on in the bronze.\n\n## Fifty-Two Days\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Joss Teague @@Ruth Achterberg:phone\nItems: ^The Tally\nWhen: +1 day\n]\n\nThe announcement came at eleven the next morning, on every screen on Lantern at once.\n\nDagny was in the canteen on Gallery Six with Joss and two of the Wick crew, trying to work out how many hours the Speakers could find between them to stand the Adeyemi girl's debt, and getting a number that was not enough. The screen on the canteen wall was usually showing either the Company's safety notices or a football match from somewhere in the Concord that had been played eleven years before and brought through the Throat on a data-run. It went dark in the middle of a goal, and then it showed the Company's mark, the two letters H and A leaning on each other like tired men, and then it showed Ruth Achterberg.\n\nThe canteen went quiet in the way Lantern went quiet for the bell.\n\nThe Director was sitting at a desk with nothing on it. She was a lean, grey woman of sixty in a dark Company suit, and she wore at her throat, on a fine chain, the brass disk of the Director's seal, which every child on Lantern had been told was the key to everything. She did not look cruel. She looked like someone who had been awake all night doing arithmetic and had come out of it with an answer she disliked but would not apologise for.\n\n\"People of the Sabra Concern,\" she said. \"I am speaking to you today with news that affects every one of us.\n\n\"As you know, the Throat which connects this system to the Concord is not a fixed thing. It breathes. Its throat-watch station has measured that breathing for the whole of this colony's history. Three weeks ago the station recorded the beginning of what our physicists call a closing phase. Throats which enter a closing phase do not reopen on any timescale that is useful to us. The best estimate, from the Concord's own literature, is somewhere between six and eight hundred years.\n\n\"The Throat will remain passable for fifty-two days. After that, this system will be cut off.\"\n\nSomebody in the canteen laughed, the high bark of a person who has been hit. Nobody else made a sound.\n\n\"The Halden-Ash Company has a duty to its shareholders and to its staff,\" said the Director. \"It cannot maintain a concern which it cannot reach. It will therefore withdraw from the Sabra system before the Throat closes. The carrier _Covenant_, now at our fore dock, will carry essential staff and their families and such Company assets as can be moved. I have asked the department heads to prepare those lists.\n\n\"I want to speak directly to the indentured population of Lantern.\" She looked into the lens. Dagny would remember that afterwards: that she did not look away. \"Your contracts are held by the Company. The Company does not abandon what it holds. Your contracts will remain in force, in trust, for the full term of the closure, and will be honoured when the Throat reopens. Lantern's systems — its air, its water, its heat, its light — will continue to be administered by the Tally, which has kept you safe for ninety years and which will keep you safe after we are gone. The Tally does not need us. It needs only that you go on working, as you always have, and it will go on keeping you alive.\n\n\"I know that this is hard news. I wish that I had better. Thank you.\"\n\nThe screen went back to the Company's mark. Then, after a moment, to the football match, which had been paused at the instant before the goal and now went on as if nothing had happened, the ball crossing the line, the crowd eleven years ago and four hundred light-years away going wild.\n\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nWhat happened in the canteen in the next ten minutes Dagny remembered afterwards only in pieces. A man on Gallery Six put his chair through the screen and the screen went on showing football in a web of cracks. Two women began to fight over nothing, over a bench, and had to be pulled apart. Old Mister Okonkwo the schoolteacher sat with his hands flat on the table, saying, \"Six hundred years, six hundred years,\" as if it were a sum that would come out differently if he said it often enough. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the fuel. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the water. Somebody shouted that they should go up to the Upper Wards now, all of them, and see how many lists the department heads had made when they had their own windows broken.\n\nAnd Pim was beside her, she didn't know when he had come in, saying, \"Mum. Mum. In trust. What's in trust mean?\"\n\nShe understood it, she realised, before she understood anything else. It was Lantern; you learned the Company's language before you learned your prayers. _Held in trust_ meant that the Company was leaving, and the debt was staying. It meant that every chit on every wrist on Lantern would go on counting down, hour by hour, against the air, for six hundred years, and that if the Throat ever opened again somebody in a Company suit would come through it with the books in their hand to see how much was owed. It meant that the Adeyemi girl's twelve hundred would be eighteen hundred by the time she was grown and nine thousand by the time she died, and would go to whoever came after her.\n\nIt meant that the lock on Gallery Nine would go on doing what it had done to Lemuel, with nobody even in the Counting House to blame for it.\n\nShe found she was standing on a table. She did not remember getting up there. The canteen was looking at her, the way the Wick crews looked at her when the lamp went wrong and someone had to say a number out loud.\n\n\"All right,\" Dagny said. Her voice came out level, which surprised her. \"All right. Shut up and listen. I'm a Speaker for the Wick crews, and from tonight the Wick crews are going to want to talk to the other crews. Pumps, farms, docks, stills. Everyone who wears a chit. Fifty-two days is a long time. It's long enough for the Company to pack. It's long enough for us to make it listen first.\"\n\n\"Listen to what?\" somebody shouted.\n\n\"To the one thing it hasn't said.\" She held up her wrist, with the chit on it, so that the canteen could see the blue numbers. \"She said the Tally keeps us alive. It doesn't. We keep it alive. We fed it ninety years. If the Company's going, the Company can take its ledger with it, or it can switch the bloody thing off before it goes. Either way, nobody on this rock is going to owe air to an empty office for six hundred years.\"\n\nThere was a silence. Then the man who had broken the screen began to clap, slowly, and others took it up; and Joss, below her, looked up with his wet red face and said, very quietly, so that only she could hear: \"Dag. You know what you just did.\"\n\nShe did. She had just volunteered.","wordCount":3670,"createdAt":1793145334280},{"id":"RV8Wzspx9OQr3q6uktJ7u","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n## Next of Kin\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"\n\n## The Bell\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Odile Marchbank @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nItems: ^The Mercy Bell\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"\n\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe _Magpie_ came in an hour later with eighty tonnes of Caul ice in her tank-frame and a crack in her forward window that had been there so long it had a name. Her pilot came through the inner lock with his helmet under his arm and the whole of the Dock Ring's gossip already on his face.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Adeyemi. Gallery Ten.\"\n\n\"Lock?\"\n\n\"Lock.\"\n\nKasimir Ostrova closed his eyes for a moment. He was nine years younger than his sister and twice as handsome and he owed money, at a conservative estimate, to a third of the Dock Ring, none of which ever seemed to cost him a night's sleep. But he had grown up in Gallery Six the same as her, and he had seen the same locks.\n\n\"Uncle Kas,\" said Pim, and was swept up and hung upside down by the ankles, which at twelve he was too old for and permitted anyway.\n\n\"How's the run?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Cold. Long. The usual.\" Kas set Pim the right way up. \"Quiet out there, though. Quieter than it was.\"\n\n\"Quiet how?\"\n\n\"Caul Deep's still shut. You know they closed the Deep in the spring — the big shaft, the one that took nine hundred on a shift. Nobody's working it. Beacon's dark, gantries are up. Funny to fly past. Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city.\" He shrugged. \"They said the vein ran out. Sent all the Deep crews home through the Throat. Contracts bought out, every one. Lucky bastards.\"\n\n\"I never heard of the Company buying out a contract in my life,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Well. You never heard of a vein running out, neither. It's a season of firsts.\" Kas grinned at Pim. \"Come on. I'll buy you a noodle. I've been paid.\"\n\n\"You've been paid and you owe Marguerite at the noodle stall forty.\"\n\n\"Then I'll buy him a noodle and her a smile, and we'll all be square.\" He put an arm round his sister's shoulders and squeezed, briefly, hard. \"Sorry about Lemuel, Dag.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Me too.\"\n\nBehind them, Odile's engraver whined on in the bronze.","wordCount":2410,"createdAt":1793145334536},{"id":"bBm1MWqIsfUqmkQ7edSJK","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"","wordCount":1433,"createdAt":1793145334699}],"tombstones":[],"blobs":[]}